WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:08.029
The first order of business when using Twinmotion is to start a project and ingest the data you want to work on.

2
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.220
Start Twinmotion. You are greeted with a home page.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:20.179
For now, go to the Templates tab and download and open the Architecture Day & Night Skies template.

4
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.620
Next, you need to ingest some data.

5
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:31.570
There are two fundamental approaches, one using a standard Export-Import procedure,

6
00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:35.960
the other using a live link between two applications.

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.659
You will learn about both methods.

8
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:46.549
Although Twinmotion can import some file formats natively, such as FBX and escapee files among others,

9
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.450
there is an advantage to using the Datasmith file format.

10
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:56.549
The Datasmith file format is better suited for fully constructed scenes like you usually have in AEC.

11
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:05.640
In both cases, you will likely need to download and install the appropriate Datasmith plugin to fit your DCC application of choice.

12
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:13.290
You can do so from the Epic Games Launcher or from the Unreal Engine Twinmotion page.

13
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:22.709
There are dedicated plugins for apps like 3ds Max, Reddit, Rhino and SketchUp to name a few.

14
00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:31.319
You install these plugins like any other Windows app and that gives you a new toolbar that you can now use in your DCC application.

15
00:01:32.489 --> 00:01:43.189
The new toolbar looks mostly identical in all DCC apps to ensure the same workflow, look and feel, regardless of the DCC application you use.

16
00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:48.290
Here I have a project in Revit that I'm planning to work on inside of Twinmotion.

17
00:01:49.269 --> 00:01:56.069
It shows a community center of sorts that includes the main building, a cafe and an open theater.

18
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:01.109
Note the Datasmith menu that was added the minute I installed the plugin.

19
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.099
This gives me access to a new toolbar.

20
00:02:04.849 --> 00:02:14.449
If all you need is a straight out export of your project, then use the Export button to export a Datasmith set to disk that you can then import into Twinmotion.

21
00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:20.319
If you'd rather use the Live Link method, then you can use the other buttons.

22
00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:23.469
Note the Connections button.

23
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.039
At this time it states that there are no connections found.

24
00:02:28.759 --> 00:02:34.159
You first need to establish a connection with Twinmotion before you can synchronize the two apps.

25
00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:38.460
In Twinmotion, use the Import panel to add an entry.

26
00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:48.250
In the dialog that appears, choose the Direct Link tab and note how it automatically picked up your Revit project.

27
00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:56.000
If you had another project opened in another app running the Live Link plugin, it would have added it to the list.

28
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:04.949
You have the option of merging objects together, based on the materials applied, or keeping them separate in a hierarchy.

29
00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:11.849
There is a third option, to combine all objects into one single mesh, but that's not very practical.

30
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.849
I'll keep the original hierarchy for this example.

31
00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:23.449
Nothing happens just yet, as all we did so far is just establish a link between the two apps.

32
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.180
Let's revisit Revit, and double check that the connection has indeed been made.

33
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.919
To see the project in Twinmotion, we only need to use the Synchronize button.

34
00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:41.229
The information is processed, and the project now appears in Twinmotion.

35
00:03:44.969 --> 00:03:52.770
From that point on, any change you make in Revit can be updated in Twinmotion simply by synchronizing the link again.

36
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:07.060
This example uses Revit as an originating source, but the workflow and end results would have been the same using other DCC applications.

37
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:13.259
The plugin and other apps will look very much like the one in Revit. Same buttons, same procedure.
